Keys and text in the order they came, and the field's serial (Istesso's ADR 0007, "変換中の文字";
ADR 0015, "文字の入力の欄"). Trial only.

- Key events and text input events travel on different objects, and a toolkit may take them in
  in another order: the Text Editor (GTK 4.14) did, seen on the host (committed text overtook
  keys typed just before it, 30 of 30 lines out of order). For a client whose text input is
  enabled, text waits after a key press (not a modifier) until the client commits its text
  input's state (it has taken the key in), and keys and buttons wait after text the same way;
  100 ms at most. Keys after keys and text after text never wait; a client without an enabled
  text input never waits. This uses the clipboard patch's queue of input (take_input,
  release_held). Seen on the host with it: the Text Editor, Firefox and Chrome 30 of 30 in order.
- wayland.text-input gains `serial`: one more (mod 256) whenever another field (the surface with
  the keyboard and its enabled text input), or the same one enabled again, has the keyboard; the
  device ends its composition when it changes.
- wayland.text-input gains state 3, "not in a field": the client has a text input on the surface
  with the keyboard and none is enabled (the device then sends plain keys, without its input
  method); 0 stays "nothing known" (no text input there). Seen on the host: the Text Editor,
  Firefox and Chrome enable and disable their text input as the focus moves by Tab, Control+L,
  Control+F and Escape, with a short "not in a field" between.
- The field is told again after every commit of a text input (the combined build had lost that
  call, so the device heard of a field only when the keyboard moved).

A patch against gst-wayland-display (https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display,
MIT License), commit 1301f86, on top of every other patch in this directory, in the order of
their names (its name sorts last on purpose). FOR TRIALS AND EVERYDAY USE ONLY; not for main
before a review.

